\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The corner branches of the centered Dirac symbol}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Extend the [free Fourier symbol](fdip-009Q) to the continuous
momentum torus #{(\mathbb R/2\pi\mathbb Z)^n}:
##{\mathcal D(p)=\ui\sum_j(\varepsilon_j^{S}\otimes\bbI_F)\sin p_j.}
Its zeros occur exactly at the #{2^n} corners
#{p^\epsilon=\pi\epsilon}, with
#{\epsilon\in\{0,1\}^n}. Near each corner,
##{\begin{aligned}
\mathcal D(p^\epsilon+k)
 &=\ui\sum_j(\varepsilon_j^{S}\otimes\bbI_F)' k_j+O(\|k\|^3),\\
(\varepsilon_j^{S}\otimes\bbI_F)'&=(-1)^{\epsilon_j}(\varepsilon_j^{S}\otimes\bbI_F),
\end{aligned}}
where the remainder is measured in the operator norm on
#{S\otimes F}. The operators #{(\varepsilon_j^{S}\otimes\bbI_F)'} obey the
same Euclidean Clifford relation as #{\varepsilon_j^{S}\otimes\bbI_F}. The leading
factor #{\ui} is the centered-difference symbol of \ref{fdip-00LZ},
not the substitution #{\varepsilon_j^{S}\mapsto\ui\varepsilon_j^{S}}.}

\proof{
\p{The identity
#{\mathcal D(p)^2=-(\sum_j\sin^2p_j)\bbI_{S\otimes F}} holds for every
real momentum. If #{\mathcal D(p)=0}, its square forces each
sine to vanish; conversely those vanishing sines make the operator
zero. Modulo #{2\pi}, each coordinate is #{0} or #{\pi}.
The expansion follows from
#{\sin(\pi\epsilon_j+k_j)=(-1)^{\epsilon_j}\sin k_j}
and #{\sin k_j=k_j+O(|k_j|^3)}. The operators #{\varepsilon_j^{S}\otimes\bbI_F} have
norm one, and there are finitely many coordinates, so summing the
remainders gives the stated operator-norm bound. Finally
##{\begin{aligned}
&(\varepsilon_i^{S}\otimes\bbI_F)'(\varepsilon_j^{S}\otimes\bbI_F)'\\
&\quad+(\varepsilon_j^{S}\otimes\bbI_F)'(\varepsilon_i^{S}\otimes\bbI_F)'
=2\delta_{ij}\bbI_{S\otimes F},
\end{aligned}}
since the two signs cancel when #{i=j}, while the off-diagonal
anticommutator was already zero.}
}

\p{Thus the centered symbol has #{2^n} local first-order
branches on the same specified spinor/internal carrier. This is
the algebraic doubling mechanism behind the sine propagator and
opposite-slope discussion in
\citet{Sections 9.1–9.2, Equations (9.6)–(9.7), (9.13)–(9.14), pp. 20–21}{wingate2025lattice}.
The finite periodic grid samples only #{2^e} of these corners
exactly; an odd period need not sample #{\pi}. Fixed boundary
twists alter the allowed momenta, as in \ref{fdip-006J}, without
removing the corners of this symbol.}

\p{The chosen #{S} is reducible, so the number of corners is
not a count relative to a minimal physical spinor representation.
Nor does the local Taylor expansion construct a quantum continuum
limit. The [precisely scoped Nielsen–Ninomiya comparison](fdip-000T)
concerns a different theorem: free local translation-invariant
Hamiltonians on an infinite odd-dimensional lattice, with isolated
relativistic zeros and compatible smooth charge projections. Under
its hypotheses the total chiral index vanishes separately for each
charge \citet{Sections 1–2, pp. 481–484; Equations (6), (10)–(11)}{friedan1982nielsen}.
The finite count and the Taylor calculation here do not
remove any of those hypotheses or supply that theorem's conclusion.}

\p{Remark. Leap §13, rung 2, names the Kähler–Dirac operator
#{\extD=\extd+\extdag} as the doubler-free comparison with this corner
count. That comparison is not proved here.}
